  DEFENDING ARMY

  Standing at the ready with my ships was I.  Lakes sailed with his loyal 
squadrons, eyes set in terrible resolution.
  The Skeletal Armies led by Babba Sissoko (#0) marched into battle with a 
fanatical fervor.  Lakes (#64) strode forward and challenged me to honorable 
combat.  I declined to enter personal combat.
  The forces commanded by me were disappointed by my faint-heartedness.  
They grimly marched on, though with lessened confidence.  As luck would have 
it, my ships were partially concealed by a sudden mist, giving them a slight 
edge.  But my careful tactical maneuvers did not succeed as expected, and my 
forces entered battle with a slight tactical disadvantage.

  On the left outside flank, the 193rd Phalanx faced the 266th Whales.  The 
266th Whales swam undersea and attacked.  The 193rd Phalanx fought the 266th 
Whales and were easily beaten by the enemy.
  In the interior front, the Veteran 195th Phalanx faced the Veteran 73rd 
Whales.  The 73rd Whales swam underwater and attacked.  The 195th Phalanx 
fought with the 73rd Whales and could not stand their ground and executed an 
orderly retreat.
  Within the interior front, the 14th Eagles faced the 111th Fleet Marines.  
The 14th Eagles soared over the 111th Fleet Marines and attacked them.  On 
this battleground, the 14th Eagles were slightly disordered by high swells 
which the local rocks seemed to channel into them and they joined the fight 
at a tiny disadvantage.  It seemed probable that the 111th Fleet Marines 
might win the battle.
  The ongoing battle blazed with a terrible fury.  The 111th Fleet Marines 
became disorganized when they were surprised by a wandering league of 
whales, saddling them with a severe disadvantage.  Even so, the 14th Eagles 
fought with the 111th Fleet Marines and could not quite hold their own 
against their savage opponent.
  In the interior front, the 98th Eagles faced the Veteran 120th Bowmen.  
The 98th Eagles swooped down on the 120th Bowmen and attacked them.  The 
98th Eagles were bothered by the fire of their feared Bowmen nemesis.  The 
98th Eagles fought the 120th Bowmen and was forced to retreat in disorder.
  In the interior lines, the 310th Trolls faced the 131st Bowmen.  On this 
field, some gossip passed through the 131st Bowmen that they would be given 
an extra share of loot if they won the battle, which bestowed upon the 131st 
Bowmen the edge.  The 310th Trolls fought the 131st Bowmen and fought 
courageously, but was steadily pushed back.
  Out on the right flank, the 161st Eagles faced the Crack 164th Skeletal 
Mermen.  The 161st Eagles swooped down on the 164th Skeletal Mermen and 
attacked without mercy.  The 164th Skeletal Mermen swam underwater and 
attacked.  The 161st Eagles battled the 164th Skeletal Mermen and was 
powerless to stop their advance.
  The fleet of Lakes finally crushed the fleet of Agesilao's morale with one 
last push.  The 266th Whales destroyed the 193rd Phalanx.  The 120th Bowmen 
annihilated the 98th Eagles.  The 131st Bowmen demolished the 310th Trolls.
  The attacking Kingdom was defeated and driven out of the Arrak Sea.  The 
battle affected the quality of our forces:  the 14th Eagles was severely 
mangled in the battle and has fallen in stature to Green condition.  I 
withdrew to Mare Ritalis with my defeated ships.  After the battle was over, 
the dismembered trolls reformed into a disgruntled band of mercenaries.
  During early summer, I attempted an illegal move.  I tried to move from 
Mare Ritalis to the Ixitxachitl Sea, a non-adjacent province.  Instead, I 
flew into the Arrak Sea.

  A fleet owing their allegiance to Agesilao invaded the province of the 
Arrak Sea, a Sea area ruled by Lord Babba Sissoko Master orango (#0).  I was 
in command of the attacking fleet.  The defenders were led by Lakes (#64).  
The armies were drawn up into lines of battle as shown here:

